  {
    "ID": 46,
    "Personaggio/soggetto": "Wilden Lightfoot",
    "Film/corto": "Onward",
    "Anno": 2020,
    "Regista/autore": "Dan Scanlon",
    "Trama": "Set in a suburban fantasy world, Disney and Pixar’s “Onward” introduces two teenage elf brothers who embark on an extraordinary quest to discover if there is still a little magic left out there. “The story is inspired by my own relationship with my brother and our connection with our dad who passed away when I was about a year old,” says director Dan Scanlon. “He’s always been a mystery to us. A family member sent us a tape recording of him saying just two words: ‘hello’ and ‘goodbye.’ Two words. But to my brother and me—it was magic.\n\n“That was the jumping-off point,” continues Scanlon. “We’ve all lost someone, and if we could spend one more day with them—what an exciting opportunity that would be. We knew that if we wanted to tell that story that we’d have to set the movie in a world where you could have that incredible opportunity.”",
    "Link 1": "Trailer: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gn5QmllRCn4",
    "Link 2": null,
    "Link 3": null,
    "Categoria Innovazione (tag) 1": "Motion",
    "Nome Innovazione 1": "Hybrid Simulation-Animation Rig",
    "Paragrafo motivazione innovazione 1": "to animate a character made up of only a pair of legs",
    "Info specifiche sul funzionamento 1": "with this rig, animators could manipulate Dad's lower half like they usually would, but the simulation team controlled everything above the belt when needed. The movement of Dad's stuffing was based on a volumetric sim, a simulation for something with volume, while the clothes on top of the stuffing were largely controlled with standard cloth simulation.\nPixar also improved the interaction between volumetric sims and cloth sims so that if you pushed on Dad's clothing, the stuffing would get squished believably, and vice versa, if you pushed on the stuffing, the clothing would move out of the way.\nThe hybrid rig improved the relationship between separate simulations, and ultimately, it showed how simulation and animation could join forces to create realistic movement on an unrealistic character.",
    "Categoria Innovazione (tag) 2": null,
    "Nome Innovazione 2": null,
    "Paragrafo Funzionamento generale 2": null,
    "Funzionamento - info maggiori su tecnologie 2": null,
    "Copertina": "assets/images/personaggi/46/copertina_46",
    "Poster": "assets/images/personaggi/46/poster_46",
    "Img01": "assets/images/personaggi/46/invz46_img_A_46",
    "Img02": "assets/images/personaggi/46/invz46_img_A_46",
    "Img03": "assets/images/personaggi/46/invz46_img_A_46",
    "Img04": "assets/images/personaggi/46/invz46_img_A_46",
    "Img05": "assets/images/personaggi/46/invz46_img_A_46",
    "Img06": "assets/images/personaggi/46/invz46_img_A_46",
    "Img07": "assets/images/personaggi/46/invz46_img_A_46",
    "Img08": "assets/images/personaggi/46/invz46_img_A_46",
    "Img09": "assets/images/personaggi/46/invz46_img_A_46",
    "Img10": "assets/images/personaggi/46/invz46_img_A_46"
  },
  {
    "ID": 47,
    "Personaggio/soggetto": "Joe",
    "Film/corto": "Soul",
    "Anno": 2020,
    "Regista/autore": "Pete Docter, Kemp Powers",
    "Trama": "“Soul” introduces Joe Gardner (voice of Jamie Foxx) – a middle-school band teacher who has a passion for jazz. “Joe wants more than anything to become a professional jazz pianist,” says director Pete Docter. “So when he’s offered a rare, once-in-a-lifetime opportunity to play with one of the greats, Joe feels he’s reached the top of the ultimate mountain.” \n\nBut one small misstep takes him from the streets of New York City to The Great Before – a fantastical place where new souls get their personalities, quirks and interests before they go to Earth. According to Docter, the idea for this unique world was 23 years in the making. “It started with my son—he’s 23 now—but the instant he was born, he already had a personality,” says Docter. “Where did that come from? I thought your personality developed through your interaction with the world. And yet, it was pretty clear that we’re all born with a very unique, specific sense of who we are.",
    "Link 1": "Trailer: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xOsLIiBStEs",
    "Link 2": null,
    "Link 3": null,
    "Categoria Innovazione (tag) 1": "Motion",
    "Nome Innovazione 1": "Finger Contact Rig",
    "Paragrafo motivazione innovazione 1": "to play instruments, especially the piano",
    "Info specifiche sul funzionamento 1": "when one of Joe's fingers pushed down on a key, the rig would calculate the correct angle for the key to rotate, so it looked like Joe was pushing it. The modeling and rigging teams also gave Joe's hand 292 controls, including variables they'd never used before, even adding a control for each tendon to convey the tension in Joe's hand as he played. This level of hand and wrist control made his performances look passionate and convincing on screen, with a level of realism that pushed the boundaries of human movement in animation.",
    "Categoria Innovazione (tag) 2": null,
    "Nome Innovazione 2": null,
    "Paragrafo Funzionamento generale 2": null,
    "Funzionamento - info maggiori su tecnologie 2": null,
    "Copertina": "assets/images/personaggi/47/copertina_47",
    "Poster": "assets/images/personaggi/47/poster_47",
    "Img01": "assets/images/personaggi/47/invz47_img_A_47",
    "Img02": "assets/images/personaggi/47/invz47_img_A_47",
    "Img03": "assets/images/personaggi/47/invz47_img_A_47",
    "Img04": "assets/images/personaggi/47/invz47_img_A_47",
    "Img05": "assets/images/personaggi/47/invz47_img_A_47",
    "Img06": "assets/images/personaggi/47/invz47_img_A_47",
    "Img07": "assets/images/personaggi/47/invz47_img_A_47",
    "Img08": "assets/images/personaggi/47/invz47_img_A_47",
    "Img09": "assets/images/personaggi/47/invz47_img_A_47",
    "Img10": "assets/images/personaggi/47/invz47_img_A_47"
  },
  {
    "ID": 48,
    "Personaggio/soggetto": "Terry and Jerry",
    "Film/corto": "Soul",
    "Anno": 2020,
    "Regista/autore": "Pete Docter, Kemp Powers",
    "Trama": "“Soul” introduces Joe Gardner (voice of Jamie Foxx) – a middle-school band teacher who has a passion for jazz. “Joe wants more than anything to become a professional jazz pianist,” says director Pete Docter. “So when he’s offered a rare, once-in-a-lifetime opportunity to play with one of the greats, Joe feels he’s reached the top of the ultimate mountain.” \n\nBut one small misstep takes him from the streets of New York City to The Great Before – a fantastical place where new souls get their personalities, quirks and interests before they go to Earth. According to Docter, the idea for this unique world was 23 years in the making. “It started with my son—he’s 23 now—but the instant he was born, he already had a personality,” says Docter. “Where did that come from? I thought your personality developed through your interaction with the world. And yet, it was pretty clear that we’re all born with a very unique, specific sense of who we are.",
    "Link 1": "Trailer: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xOsLIiBStEs",
    "Link 2": null,
    "Link 3": null,
    "Categoria Innovazione (tag) 1": "Motion",
    "Nome Innovazione 1": "Ground Locking ",
    "Paragrafo motivazione innovazione 1": "to mantain charcaters \"glued\" to the ground, avoiding overlapping errors or unnatural movements",
    "Info specifiche sul funzionamento 1": "it's a procedural animation system that automatically aligns the 3D model to a defined path, adapting the character's position and orientation to uneven grounds",
    "Categoria Innovazione (tag) 2": "Motion",
    "Nome Innovazione 2": "2D CoDE",
    "Paragrafo Funzionamento generale 2": "to capture the extreme flexibility of Characters like Terry and Jerry that are made of simple 2D lines",
    "Funzionamento - info maggiori su tecnologie 2": "it automates movements",
    "Copertina": "assets/images/personaggi/48/copertina_48",
    "Poster": "assets/images/personaggi/48/poster_48",
    "Img01": "assets/images/personaggi/48/invz48_img_A_48",
